# Trivy ignore file for AegisGate Security Platform v3.3.0
# Format: https://aquasecurity.github.io/trivy/v0.71/docs/suppression/
#
# Each line is an ID to suppress. To suppress a specific CVE/misconfig in a
# specific file, use: <ID>:<path> with # comment on the same or next line.
#
# See: legal-docs/21-self-attestation-v3.3.0/security-posture-v3.3.0.md
# Section 4.5 for the original review and remediation plan.

# === Secret findings: 3 RSA private keys in upstream/aegisgate/pkg/adapters/certs/ ===
#
# Reason: TEST FIXTURES for the platform's TLS adapter self-tests (verify
# AegisGate TLS handshake, certificate validation, and CA-signed certificate
# chain verification logic). They are intentionally checked into the
# upstream source for self-test reproducibility.
#
# Important:
# - The `upstream/` directory is NOT part of the v3.2.0 release code. It is
#   consolidated source from the AegisGate and AegisGuard upstream repos,
#   used as a reference for the active platform code.
# - These private keys are FOR TEST USE ONLY. They MUST NOT be used to sign
#   production certificates, establish production TLS, or be deployed in
#   production environments.
# - The platform's runtime does not load these keys; they are only used by
#   Go unit tests in the adapters package.
# - The `upstream/` directory is being progressively deprecated as the
#   active platform code matures. v3.4.0+ plans to remove `upstream/`
#   entirely, at which point these ignores can be deleted.

# T-S01 — RSA private key in upstream/aegisgate/pkg/adapters/certs/server.key
# Trivy reports this as: secret:RSA private key (trivy classifies as private-key)
# We suppress by file:line since trivy doesn't have a stable secret ID.
# Trivy 0.71 docs recommend using a custom Rego policy for path-based secret
# suppression. For v3.3.0, we document the suppressions here as a comment;
# the active suppression is via the Rego policy in trivy-policy.rego below.

# === Suppression policy (Rego) ===
# Trivy supports Rego-based suppression via --ignore-policy flag.
# This file documents WHY each finding is accepted as a known false positive
# for the v3.3.0 self-attestation. The actual suppression happens at CI
# runtime via the .trivy/rego/policy.rego file (not yet created; planned
# for v3.3.1).
